        "end_line": 206,
        "side": "new",
        "severity": "high",
        "category": "bug",
        "title": "MOV r/m64, imm32 now stores only 4 bytes to memory instead of 8",
        "explanation": "Previously `mov_rm_imm!(u32tou64)` sign-extended the imm32 to a u64 and wrote 8 bytes (`imm.to_le_bytes()` on a u64). Now the macro passes `size_of::<u32>()` = 4 to set_op, so for a memory destination (e.g. `mov qword [rax], -1`, REX.W C7 /0) only the low 4 bytes are written and the upper 4 bytes keep stale guest memory. That silently corrupts guest data on MMIO/emulated memory writes.",
        "suggestion": "Use an operand size of 8 for the destination of Mov_rm64_imm32: read the imm with 4 bytes and write with 8 (e.g. give mov_rm_imm separate source/destination size parameters, or call it with u64 if get_op sign-extends Immediate32to64). Add a test for the memory form.",
        "evidence": "    mov_rm_imm!(u32);",
        "confidence": 0.7,
        "id": "210106d5d029",
